            {
                "entry_id": "fbda13d9-764f-33f3-a97f-4be9533bf21f",
                "word": "Cisalpine",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "a3589b89-7388-4413-836d-eef773d21cee",
                        "entry_id": "fbda13d9-764f-33f3-a97f-4be9533bf21f",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Cisalpines"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "401b5b63-90a6-3fce-912a-d0ba44bd3c98",
                        "entry_id": "fbda13d9-764f-33f3-a97f-4be9533bf21f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of Gallican (“an adherent to, and supporter of, Gallicanism”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4ddc8b62-7f87-386d-ab7f-3f94dee1311d",
                        "entry_id": "fbda13d9-764f-33f3-a97f-4be9533bf21f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In 18th–19th century Britain, a Roman Catholic opponent of ultramontanism and advocate of Catholic emancipation through compromise and obedience to secular authorities."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4c8600ea-7242-4f18-b14e-8055c48a4543",
                                "sense_id": "4ddc8b62-7f87-386d-ab7f-3f94dee1311d",
                                "text": "Some of the Cisalpines carried their opposition to the monastic orders so far as to be very unfriendly to the French émigrées nuns, for whose expulsion from England Sir J. Mildmay introduced a Bill in 1800."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6d59ab35-4a45-4759-903f-0735b649c7a7",
                                "sense_id": "4ddc8b62-7f87-386d-ab7f-3f94dee1311d",
                                "text": "But the Cisalpines began to be overshadowed by the ultramontane spirit in the 1830s, and it was not until after the Second Vatican Council a hundred and thirty years later that English Roman Catholics again asked themselves whether they were primarily English or primarily Roman."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b44a836c-5053-472d-a8cb-9d5529095239",
                                "sense_id": "4ddc8b62-7f87-386d-ab7f-3f94dee1311d",
                                "text": "Just as with the Blackloists and Jansenists (and perhaps even more so), suspicion of the supernatural came to characterize the Cisalpines, although this time it had little to do with their doctrine of grace and more to do with the Cisalpines’ desire to make Catholicism comprehensible to a sceptical age."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
